{
  "result": "The old lighthouse on the edge of town never actually guided ships. There was no ocean for a hundred miles, just wheat fields that rolled like a golden sea under the wind. But it stood there anyway, stubborn and whitewashed, built by a man named Silas who missed the coast so badly he decided to invent his own.\n\nEvery evening at six, Silas climbed the spiral stairs and lit the lamp. The farmers joked about it at first, then they got used to it, then they started to depend on it. Kids used it to find their way home after playing in the fields late. Couples met under its beam. On stormy nights, when the rain hammered the plains and the sky turned ink-black, that light was the only thing that made the world feel less enormous.\n\nSilas died twenty years ago. No one ever told the lighthouse. A neighbor's boy, now a man with his own children, still climbs those stairs. He doesn't know why either, not really. He just knows that some lights are not about being seen by ships, but about proving that someone is still awake, still caring, still hoping in the dark.\n\nWe all build lighthouses inland, in a way. We maintain rituals, relationships, and kindnesses that might seem pointless to an outsider. But someone, somewhere, is using your light to find their way home, even if you never knew they were lost.\n\nSo keep the lamp lit tonight and always without question my friend.",
